## Installing Python

means putting the interpreter onto your machine so it can actually run code
get it from python.org — has installers for windows and mac
windows: tick the "Add Python to PATH" checkbox during install, otherwise your terminal won't recognise the python command
mac: sometimes has an old system version already on there that isn't meant for you to use, install your own instead:
bash
  brew install python
linux: usually comes preinstalled already
check it worked:
bash
  python --version
  python3 --version

## Python interpreter

the program that reads your code and runs it, line by line
different from something like C++ where the whole program gets compiled into machine code before anything runs
python reads and executes as it goes — that's why it's called an interpreted language
the standard version everyone uses is called CPython
two ways to use it:
type code straight into it interactively
point it at a file and let it run the whole thing top to bottom

## Running .py files

.py is the file extension for a plain python script
open a terminal in the same folder the file's in and run:
bash
  python filename.py
example — a file called hello.py containing:
python
  print("Hello, world!")

running it prints that line straight to the terminal

clicking a "run" button in an editor does this exact same thing, just without typing the command yourself

## REPL

stands for Read-Eval-Print Loop, python's interactive mode
type python with no filename after it and you get a >>> prompt
type one line at a time, see the result immediately:
  >>> 2 + 2
  4
good for quick tests, but nothing typed in there gets saved anywhere
exit with exit(), or ctrl+d on mac/linux, ctrl+z then enter on windows

## Terminal

a text-only way of controlling your computer instead of clicking through folders and icons
also called command line, command prompt, shell — all the same idea
windows: Command Prompt or PowerShell
mac/linux: Terminal
most code editors have one built in too
commands you'll use constantly:
cd foldername — move into a folder
cd .. — move back up a folder
ls (or dir on windows) — list what's in the current folder
pwd — check which folder you're currently in

## VS Code

a free code editor from microsoft, downloadable at code.visualstudio.com
on its own it's just a text editor, but extensions turn it into a proper setup for python
comes with syntax highlighting, autocomplete, a run button
has a terminal built right in — open with ctrl + backtick, or View → Terminal

## Python extensions

extensions add the actual python functionality to VS Code — on its own it doesn't know anything special about python
the one to grab: Python, made by microsoft
pulls in Pylance alongside it automatically for smarter autocomplete and error checking
install by:
clicking the extensions icon in the sidebar (or ctrl+shift+x)
searching "python"
hitting install

## python vs python3

on some machines, typing python opens an old version, while python3 gives the one you actually want
other machines point python straight at the newer version already
check both instead of guessing:
bash
  python --version
  python3 --version
use whichever one gives back a 3.x result

## pip

python's built in package manager — pulls in code other people have written so you're not building everything from zero
comes bundled with python automatically
commands you'll actually use:
bash
  pip install requests
  pip list
  pip uninstall requests
  pip install --upgrade pip
everything it installs comes from PyPI, a massive public library of free python packages

## Python versions

version numbers follow major.minor.micro, e.g. 3.12.4
major (2 → 3): big changes, old code isn't guaranteed to still work
minor (3.11 → 3.12): new features, generally stays backward compatible
micro (3.12.1 → 3.12.2): small bug and security fixes, always safe to update
matters because some syntax or libraries only work on certain versions
check yours with:
bash
  python --version

## pipenv

worth not mixing up with pyenv — pyenv manages which python version you're running, pipenv manages packages and virtual environments for a specific project
basically merges pip and virtualenv into one tool
instead of a requirements.txt file, it creates:
a Pipfile — human readable, lists what your project depends on
a Pipfile.lock — exact versions actually installed, so someone else pulling your project gets identical results
commands you'd use:
bash
  pipenv install requests
  pipenv install pytest --dev
  pipenv shell
  pipenv run python script.py
pipenv install grabs a package and adds it to the Pipfile
--dev flag is for packages only needed while developing (like testing tools), not to run the project itself
pipenv shell drops you into the project's virtual environment
pipenv run runs something inside that environment without fully activating it first